/// OTP supervisor restart strategy — how sibling children are affected when
/// one of them crashes.
type SupervisionStrategy = #oneForOne | #oneForAll | #restForOne
/// Supervisor — Abstract base class for static OTP supervision trees.
///
/// Subclass `Supervisor` to define a static supervision tree: one whose
/// children are known at startup. Override `childSpecs` to return the list
/// of child classes (or `SupervisionSpec` values for per-child
/// configuration). All other configuration methods have sensible defaults
/// that can be selectively overridden.
///
/// ## Configuration runs inside the supervisor's `init`
///
/// Configuration (`childSpecs`, `strategy`, `maxRestarts`, `restartWindow`)
/// is instance-side (ADR 0135 §2): the runtime sends each setting to the
/// supervisor being started, from inside its own OTP `init/1`. So `self` is
/// that supervisor and `self args` its start args (`nil` for `supervise`).
/// The rules:
///
/// - **Do not send messages to `self`** from a configuration method
///   (`self count`, `self which: X`): the supervisor is still starting, and
///   the start fails with `#supervisor_start_failed`. Reading `self args` and
///   passing `self` *as a value* (e.g. in a child's args) are fine.
/// - **Never synchronously call a supervisor from code it is waiting on**:
///   its configuration methods, those of its nested supervisor children, or
///   the `initialize` of any actor child. A call to an ancestor from there
///   hangs during start, every OTP restart and every `DynamicSupervisor
///   startChild:` (from a child's `initialize`, until the 5 s start timeout,
///   which then fails the start). Make a child's first call to its parent
///   from a `continueWith:` step, which runs after its start has returned.
/// - **A child's `initialize` completes before its start returns** (BT-4033):
///   children start in order, each waiting for its `initialize`, as OTP
///   `init/1` does. An `initialize` that raises fails the supervisor's start
///   (`#supervisor_start_failed`, with the child's error in the hint)
///   instead of the child crash-looping after the start reported success.
///   Put slow setup in a `continueWith:` step.
/// - **A start made from inside one of the class's own class methods**
///   (`class make => self spawnWith: …`, or `self supervise`) runs in that
///   class's process, like any class method body. Its configuration must not
///   send to that same class (`MySup settings`): the class is busy and the
///   send fails. Starts from anywhere else run in the caller, so there such
///   sends are fine.
///
/// Each setting is a separate send, so put expensive work (reading files,
/// say) in `childSpecs` only.
///
/// See ADR 0059 and ADR 0135 for the full design.
///
/// ## Examples
/// ```beamtalk
/// Supervisor subclass: WebApp
///   childSpecs => #(DatabasePool HTTPRouter MetricsCollector)
///
/// app := (WebApp supervise) unwrap
/// app count   // => 3
/// ```
///
/// @see DynamicSupervisor (for runtime-added children)
/// @see Actor (base class for supervised processes)
/// @see SupervisionSpec (for per-child configuration)
abstract Object subclass: Supervisor

  // === Class-side ===

  /// Whether this class is a supervisor (always true for Supervisor subclasses).
  ///
  /// Used by `SupervisionSpec childSpec` to determine `type` and `shutdown`.
  class isSupervisor -> Boolean => true

  /// Default OTP restart policy when this supervisor is nested in another
  /// (ADR 0135 §3): `#permanent`, OTP's default for supervisor children.
  /// Override to declare `#transient` or `#temporary`.
  class supervisionPolicy -> RestartStrategy => #permanent

  /// Return a SupervisionSpec for nesting this supervisor in another.
  ///
  /// The spec's `type` is `#supervisor` and its shutdown `#infinity`; its
  /// restart comes from `supervisionPolicy`. `withId:` (a Symbol or an
  /// Integer), `withArgs:` (this child's start args, read as `self args`),
  /// `withRestart:`, `withShutdown:` and `withName:` (registers the child
  /// under a bounded name) apply. `withClassMethod:` and `scope: #global`
  /// are not supported for supervisor children: they fail the parent's
  /// start with a `#type_error`. A bare class in `childSpecs` means
  /// `X supervisionSpec`, so it is an anonymous child.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// Supervisor subclass: Pool
  ///   childSpecs => #((Leaf supervisionSpec withId: 1), (Leaf supervisionSpec withId: 2 withArgs: #{#x => 2}))
  /// ```
  class supervisionSpec -> SupervisionSpec =>
    (SupervisionSpec new withActorClass: ((Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) classObject: self)) withRestart: self supervisionPolicy

  // === Class-side lifecycle ===
  //
  // `supervise` *ensures* the class's one registered instance is running;
  // the spawn family *creates* a new instance per call (ADR 0135 §1). All of
  // them start the tree in the calling process, unlinked from it (like
  // `Actor spawn`): to tie a supervisor's lifetime to something, nest it via
  // `supervisionSpec` or `stop` it. Instance-side `initialize` runs once per
  // start, in the caller. An ordinary send of these messages is routed by
  // the compiler and runtime straight to the start helper; the bodies below
  // document the behaviour and serve the remaining dynamic paths. They are
  // `sealed` because an override would never run on those routed sends.

  /// Start this supervisor (or return the already-running instance).
  ///
  /// Registers the supervisor under its class name. Subsequent calls to
  /// `supervise` or `current` return the running instance. Start args are
  /// `nil`; use `spawnWith:` for an instance with args.
  ///
  /// ADR 0080 Phase 2 (BT-1999): returns `Result(Self, Error)` so subclass
  /// typing is preserved (e.g. `MySupervisor supervise unwrap` is typed as
  /// `MySupervisor`, not the abstract `Supervisor` base). Use `unwrap` for
  /// boot-style "crash on failure" call sites, or `ifOk:ifError:` /
  /// `andThen:` for recoverable starts. Failures surface as
  /// `Result error: #beamtalk_error{kind = supervisor_start_failed}`,
  /// including an `initialize` that raises and a call that waited too long
  /// behind a concurrent `supervise` of the same class (concurrent calls are
  /// served in arrival order).
  class sealed supervise -> Result(Self, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) startLink: self

  /// Start a new, anonymous instance of this supervisor with `nil` args.
  ///
  /// Each call starts a separate tree. Raises `#supervisor_start_failed`
  /// when the start fails; an error raised by `initialize` propagates as
  /// is. Not `current`: look an anonymous instance up by
  /// the handle you hold, or `Node current supervisors`.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// a := WebApp spawn
  /// b := WebApp spawn
  /// a == b      // => false
  /// a stop      // b keeps running
  /// ```
  class sealed spawn -> Self => (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) doSpawn: self

  /// Start a new, anonymous instance with start args (`self args`).
  ///
  /// The configuration methods read `args` inside the new supervisor's
  /// `init`; an OTP restart replays the same args. Raises
  /// `#supervisor_start_failed` when the start fails.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// srv := ServerSupervisor spawnWith: #{#port => 8080}
  /// srv args at: #port   // => 8080
  /// ```
  class sealed spawnWith: args :: Object -> Self =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) doSpawnWith: self args: args

  /// Start a new instance registered under `name` (`nil` args).
  ///
  /// `name` must come from a bounded, statically known set (ADR 0079): it is
  /// an atom. A taken name gives `Result error:` with kind
  /// `#name_registered`; any other failure, including an `initialize` that
  /// raises, `#supervisor_start_failed` (as `Actor spawnAs:` answers a
  /// failed `initialize` in its `Result`).
  class sealed spawnAs: name :: Symbol -> Result(Self, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) doSpawnAs: self withName: name

  /// Start a new instance with start args, registered under `name`.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// http := (ServerSupervisor spawnWith: cfg as: #http_server) unwrap
  /// (ServerSupervisor named: #http_server) unwrap == http   // => true
  /// ```
  class sealed spawnWith: args :: Object as: name :: Symbol -> Result(Self, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor)
      doSpawnWith: self
      args: args
      withName: name

  /// Look up a running supervisor registered under `name`.
  ///
  /// `Result error:` with kind `#name_not_registered` for an unknown name,
  /// `#wrong_class` when the process is not a supervisor of this class or a
  /// subclass (an actor, say), `#type_error` for a non-Symbol name.
  class named: name :: Symbol -> Result(Self, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) named: self withName: name

  /// Return the running `supervise` instance, or nil if not started.
  ///
  /// Only the class-named singleton: instances from the spawn family (and
  /// supervisors nested as a bare class) are not `current`.
  class current -> Self | Nil => (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) current: self

  // === Instance-side configuration (override in concrete subclasses) ===
  //
  // Read from the supervisor being started, inside its `init/1` (ADR 0135).

  /// Return the list of child classes (or SupervisionSpec values) for this supervisor.
  ///
  /// Must be overridden by concrete subclasses. Raises SubclassResponsibility otherwise.
  /// Runs inside the supervisor's own `init`: use `self args` for start args
  /// and pass `self` as a value if a child needs its parent, but do not send
  /// messages to `self` (see the class comment).
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// Supervisor subclass: WebApp
  ///   childSpecs => #(DatabasePool HTTPRouter)
  /// ```
  childSpecs -> List => self subclassResponsibility

  /// OTP supervisor restart strategy.
  ///
  /// Valid values: `#oneForOne`, `#oneForAll`, `#restForOne`.
  /// Default: `#oneForOne`.
  strategy -> SupervisionStrategy => #oneForOne

  /// Maximum number of restarts within `restartWindow` seconds.
  ///
  /// If this rate is exceeded, the supervisor itself shuts down.
  maxRestarts -> Integer => 10

  /// Time window in seconds for the `maxRestarts` rate limit.
  restartWindow -> Integer => 60

  /// The start args this supervisor was started with: `nil` for `supervise`.
  ///
  /// OTP restarts replay the same args, so a restarted supervisor recomputes
  /// the same configuration. Readable from the configuration methods (inside
  /// `init`) and from outside; raises with kind `#stale_handle` when the
  /// supervisor is no longer running, and `#node_down` when a remote
  /// supervisor's node cannot be reached.
  args -> Dynamic => self argsResult unwrap

  /// Internal FFI seam (ADR 0101 Part 4): the raw `Result`-shaped `args/1` call.
  internal argsResult => (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) args: self

  /// Lifecycle hook called after all children have started.
  ///
  /// Override to perform post-start wiring (e.g., discovering siblings
  /// via `which:` or injecting dependencies). `self` is the new supervisor.
  /// Runs in the caller's process after the supervisor has finished
  /// starting, so `which:` and other messages to `self` work here (unlike
  /// in the configuration methods). If it raises, the supervisor is stopped
  /// and the start fails: `spawn` and `spawnWith:` re-raise the error,
  /// while `supervise`, `spawnAs:` and `spawnWith:as:` answer `Result error:`
  /// with kind `#supervisor_start_failed` (the original error is its cause).
  ///
  /// Fires exactly once per top-level start — the `supervise` call that
  /// started the instance, or each `spawn`/`spawnWith:`/`spawnAs:` — not for
  /// a supervisor nested in another (ADR 0135 §1). It does
  /// **not** re-fire on OTP-driven child restarts (including
  /// `restForOne` cascades), since those start each child directly
  /// from its child spec and never re-enter this dispatch path. A
  /// dependency reference that must survive a restart needs named
  /// actor registration (see ADR 0079) instead of one-shot wiring here.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// Supervisor subclass: App
  ///   childSpecs => #(Store, Engine)
  ///   initialize =>
  ///     engine := (self which: Engine) unwrap
  ///     store := (self which: Store) unwrap
  ///     engine connectStore: store
  /// ```
  initialize -> Nil => nil

  // === Instance-side inspection ===
  //
  // Every method below talks to this supervisor instance through its handle
  // (its pid), never through the class: they work the same on the
  // `supervise` singleton, on any instance from the spawn family, and on a
  // nested supervisor reached through `childWithId:` or `children`.

  /// Return the running children as objects, in declaration order.
  ///
  /// Each element is the live child: an Actor, or a Supervisor /
  /// DynamicSupervisor handle for a nested supervisor. Children that are
  /// restarting or stopped are left out; `whichChildren` lists every spec.
  ///
  /// **Changed in BT-3966:** this used to return the child ids as Symbols.
  /// For the ids use `whichChildren collect: [:c | c id]`.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// app children size                   // => 2
  /// app children first ping             // => #pong
  /// app whichChildren collect: [:c | c id]   // => #(#db, #router)
  /// ```
  children -> List(Object) => self whichChildrenResult unwrap

  /// Internal FFI seam (ADR 0101 Part 4): the raw `Result`-shaped
  /// `whichChildren/1` call. Keeps `children` pure Beamtalk.
  internal whichChildrenResult -> Result(List(Object), Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) whichChildren: self

  /// Return one `SupervisorChild` per child spec, in declaration order.
  ///
  /// Includes children that OTP is restarting (`#restarting`) and ones that
  /// were terminated but whose spec is still held (`#stopped`), both with
  /// `child` nil. Raises with kind `#stale_handle` when the supervisor is no
  /// longer running.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// (app whichChildren collect: [:c | c id])       // => #(#listener, #acceptorPool)
  /// (app whichChildren detect: [:c | c isRunning]) child
  /// ```
  whichChildren -> List(SupervisorChild) => self childInfosResult unwrap

  /// Internal FFI seam (ADR 0101 Part 4): the raw `Result`-shaped
  /// `childInfos/1` call.
  internal childInfosResult -> Result(List(SupervisorChild), Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) childInfos: self

  /// Look up the running child with the given OTP child id.
  ///
  /// The id is the child's class name by default, or the value given to
  /// `SupervisionSpec withId:` (a Symbol or an Integer). Returns the child
  /// for a running child; `Result ok: nil` when the id exists but the child
  /// is restarting or stopped; `Result error:` with kind `#child_not_found`
  /// for an unknown id, `#type_error` for an id that is not a Symbol or an
  /// Integer, `#stale_handle` when the supervisor is not running.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// listener := (server childWithId: #listener) unwrap
  /// (server childWithId: #nope) error kind   // => #child_not_found
  /// ```
  childWithId: anId :: Symbol | Integer -> Result(Object, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) childWithId: self id: anId

  /// Terminate the child with the given OTP child id.
  ///
  /// Idempotent (ADR 0080): an unknown or already-terminated id returns
  /// `Result ok: nil`, since a terminated `#temporary` child's spec is gone.
  /// A `#permanent` or `#transient` child keeps its spec (`#stopped` in
  /// `whichChildren`) until `restartChild:` or `deleteChild:`. Other
  /// failures give `#terminate_failed`; a non-Symbol, non-Integer id
  /// `#type_error`; a stopped supervisor `#stale_handle`. Because of the
  /// idempotence, a misspelled id also answers `ok: nil`: check ids with
  /// `childWithId:` (`#child_not_found`) where that matters.
  ///
  /// Never send this (or `terminate:`) from the child being terminated: the
  /// supervisor waits for that child to stop while the child waits for the
  /// supervisor's reply, so the call hangs until the child's shutdown
  /// timeout kills it.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// (server terminateChild: #acceptorPool) unwrap   // suspend
  /// ```
  terminateChild: anId :: Symbol | Integer -> Result(Nil, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) terminateChildId: self id: anId

  /// Restart a terminated child by its OTP child id; returns the new child.
  ///
  /// Only a `#permanent` or `#transient` child can be restarted: OTP removes
  /// a `#temporary` child's spec (the default restart policy) when it
  /// terminates, so `restartChild:` then gives `#child_not_found`. A child
  /// that is running or restarting gives `#child_running`; a failing start
  /// `#child_start_failed`.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// (server terminateChild: #acceptorPool) unwrap
  /// pool := (server restartChild: #acceptorPool) unwrap   // resume
  /// (server restartChild: #acceptorPool) error kind       // => #child_running
  /// ```
  restartChild: anId :: Symbol | Integer -> Result(Object, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) restartChild: self id: anId

  /// Delete the spec of a terminated child, so `whichChildren` no longer
  /// lists it.
  ///
  /// A running or restarting child gives `#child_running` (terminate it
  /// first); an unknown id `#child_not_found`.
  ///
  /// ## Examples
  /// ```beamtalk
  /// (server terminateChild: #acceptor_3) unwrap
  /// (server deleteChild: #acceptor_3) unwrap
  /// ```
  deleteChild: anId :: Symbol | Integer -> Result(Nil, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) deleteChild: self id: anId

  /// Look up the running child process for the given class.
  ///
  /// A convenience for trees with one child per class: when several children
  /// share the class, it returns `Result error:` with kind `#ambiguous_child`
  /// (use `childWithId:` then). Matches by the child's class, so a child
  /// declared `withId:` is found too.
  ///
  /// ADR 0080 Phase 2 (BT-2041): returns `Result(Object, Error)`. The ok
  /// variant spans `Object | nil` — a class with no running child returns
  /// `Result ok: nil`, not an error. Genuine failures (e.g. supervisor
  /// process dead) surface as `Result error: #beamtalk_error{kind = stale_handle}`.
  which: aClass :: Class -> Result(Object, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) whichChild: self class: aClass

  /// Terminate the running child process for the given class.
  ///
  /// The child is found by its class, so one declared `withId:` is
  /// terminated too. When several children share the class nothing is
  /// terminated and the result is `Result error:` with kind
  /// `#ambiguous_child`: use `terminateChild:` with an id then.
  ///
  /// ADR 0080 Phase 2 (BT-1999): returns `Result(Nil, Error)`. Idempotent
  /// on `not_found` — terminating an already-gone child returns `Result ok: nil`.
  /// Genuine failures surface as
  /// `Result error: #beamtalk_error{kind = terminate_failed}`.
  terminate: aClass :: Class -> Result(Nil, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) terminateChild: self class: aClass

  /// Return the count of currently-running children.
  ///
  /// BT-1997 / ADR 0080 Phase 1: `countChildren/1` returns a Result-shaped
  /// value; call `unwrap` at the FFI boundary to preserve the pre-migration
  /// user-facing signature.
  count -> Integer => self countChildrenResult unwrap

  /// Internal FFI seam (ADR 0101 Part 4): the raw `Result`-shaped
  /// `countChildren/1` call. Keeps `count` pure Beamtalk.
  internal countChildrenResult -> Result(Integer, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) countChildren: self

  /// Is this supervisor running?
  ///
  /// A supervisor on another node answers `true` (as an actor does); the
  /// next call to it reports `#stale_handle` or `#node_down`. Sealed, like
  /// `stop` (BT-3984): the runtime answers both without a method lookup.
  ///
  /// WARNING: check-then-act is racy; the supervisor can stop right after.
  sealed isAlive -> Boolean => (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) isAlive: self

  /// Stop this supervisor and all its children.
  ///
  /// Raises with kind `#stale_handle` when the supervisor is not running and
  /// `#node_down` when its node cannot be reached. Sealed (BT-3984).
  ///
  /// BT-1997 / ADR 0080 Phase 1: `stop/1` returns a Result-shaped value;
  /// call `unwrap` at the FFI boundary to preserve the pre-migration
  /// user-facing signature.
  sealed stop -> Nil => self stopResult unwrap

  /// Internal FFI seam (ADR 0101 Part 4): the raw `Result`-shaped `stop/1`
  /// call. Keeps `stop` pure Beamtalk.
  internal stopResult -> Result(Nil, Error) =>
    (Erlang beamtalk_supervisor) stop: self
